*  TUPLE_driver - exercises TUPLE(OP,ARG1,ARG2,ARG3) and the register-file
*  helpers LOAD, STORE, ADDR, INDEX, REG, FREE, ISREG, ASSOC and DEASSOC.
*
*  NOTE ON THE CONTRACT.  TUPLE.sno is the ONLY module in this batch that
*  ships with NO header comment, so it has no stated contract to write cases
*  from.  What is documented lives one file over: ASM.sno's header names the
*  machine M of chapter 18 of "Algorithms in SNOBOL4" and its LIST gives the
*  opcode set -- LOAD STORE ADD FADD SUB FSUB MUL FMUL DIV FDIV LOADA LOADN
*  BR BRGT BRLT BREQ BRNE BRGE BRLE.  TUPLE emits code for that same machine,
*  so the cases below are one tuple per documented opcode family, driven
*  through the published signature only.  They are NOT read off TUPLE.sno's
*  implementation, and the register bookkeeping is exercised only as those
*  tuples happen to drive it.
*
*  ORACLE NOTE.  This .ref comes from CSNOBOL4 (snobol4 -b), not from
*  x64/bin/sbl.  Spitbol REJECTS the module outright -- TUPLE.sno(31) :
*  ERROR 248 -- attempted redefinition of system function, raised on
*  DEFINE('LOAD(LOC)') because LOAD is a Spitbol built-in -- so no sbl run
*  can produce anything but an error dump carrying execution time and
*  memory figures.  CSNOBOL4 is the other sanctioned SNOBOL4 oracle and
*  takes the module as written.
-INCLUDE "TUPLE.sno"
        TUPLE('LBL', 'START')
        TUPLE('ASGN', 'A', , 'B')
        TUPLE('ADD', 'A', 'B', 'C')
        TUPLE('SUB', 'C', 'A', 'D')
        TUPLE('MUL', 'C', 'D', 'E')
        TUPLE('DIV', 'E', 'B', 'F')
        TUPLE('FADD', 'A', 'B', 'G')
        TUPLE('MNS', 'G', , 'H')
        TUPLE('BRGT', 'H', 'A', 'START')
        TUPLE('BREQ', 'H', '10', 'START')
        TUPLE('BR', , , 'START')
END
